\chapter{PEG Semantic Values and AST Construction}
\label{ch:peg-semantic-values}

\section{From Matches to Trees}

The two PEG layers of \DSLtk{} produce matches: a span of text, the rule that
recognised it, the channel it was routed into, and a position. What they do
not produce is a tree. This is not an oversight in the implementation but a
division of labour that the design makes explicit: the matching layer
answers \emph{what was matched here}, and the question \emph{what does the
whole thing mean} is the user's, to be answered by a semantic action.

That answer has three possible shapes, and \DSLtk{} offers all three, at
different levels of convenience. A hand-written recursive-descent parser
built on \code{dsl::Parser<T>} makes the semantic value the parser's own
result type, and the tree is assembled by the parser as it goes. A PEG
grammar with actions makes the semantic value something the action
manufactures from the match, usually by pushing and popping on a stack that
the action closes over. The Earley parser of \chapref{ch:cfg-earley} builds
the tree itself, as a by-product of the recognition algorithm, because the
algorithm has to keep the partial derivations anyway.

This chapter is about the middle option, and it is deliberately candid about
its difficulties. The action model of the PEG layer is the simplest of the
three to write and the most awkward to scale, and the reasons why are
structural rather than incidental.

\section{The Match Record}

Everything an action can see is in \code{PEGMatch}, a plain aggregate with
nine members and one method.

