The type of an action is \code{PEGSemanticAction}, an alias for
\code{std::function<void (PEGMatch &)>}. It is attached to a rule through
\code{PEGRule::semantic_action}, or supplied at the call site as the
callback of a matcher arm.

Three properties of the match record shape everything that follows.

The \code{value} view is not owned. In the definition layer it is a
substring of the \code{std::string_view} that was passed to \code{parse},
so it points into the caller's buffer. The header describes
it as valid only for the duration of the parse that produced it, which is
conservative: it is valid exactly as long as the buffer the caller supplied
is alive. If the caller passes a temporary \code{std::string}, the view
dangles as soon as the parse returns, and an action that stores it -- in a
tree, say -- stores a dangling pointer. Storing \code{std::string(m.value)}
is the safe move, and it is what a tree-building action must do.

The \code{rule} pointer is the only way back to the grammar. A match does
not carry a tag, a name, or any other description of the rule beyond the
pointer, so an action that wants to know \emph{which} kind of token it has
seen must either compare the pointer against a rule it captured, or compare
\code{m.value} against a string. Both work; the first is faster and the
second is more robust, since a rule pointer is only stable while the
grammar is not being modified.

The \code{user_data} slot is null on entry and is never read by the
subsystem. Section~\ref{sec:ch27-userdata} returns to it.

\section{The Action Model}

An action runs when its rule matches. In the definition layer it is
\code{PEGDefinition::parse} that fires it, and the conditions are precise:
the action runs unless the rule is negated and the rule's \code{fire_on_neg}
flag is false. In the streaming matcher the action is the callback of the
arm that fired; if the arm has no callback of its own, the rule's own
semantic action is used instead.

What the action can do is the subject of this section, and the answer has
three parts.

First, it can read the match. This is the ordinary case: the action reads
\code{m.value}, or \code{m.length()}, or the position, and uses what it
finds.

Second, it can write to \code{m.user_data}. The slot exists for this
purpose, and writing to it is well defined -- it is a plain
\code{void *}. What writing to it does \emph{not} do is carry a value to the
next match; Section~\ref{sec:ch27-userdata} explains why, and it is the
single most common misconception about this layer.

Third, it can mutate the match. The action receives a non-const reference,
so it may change \code{m.value}, \code{m.begin}, \code{m.end},
\code{m.channel} or \code{m.ignored}. Those changes are, however, local to
the action. The definition layer discards the match as soon as the action
returns, and the streaming matcher sets the cursor \emph{before} it runs the
action, so a match whose \code{end} was changed does not move the cursor.
Mutating a match is therefore a way of computing something and stowing it
elsewhere, not a way of influencing the parse.

That last point is the defining limitation of the whole model: an action
fires \emph{after} the match is known. It cannot influence whether the rule
matches, how much it consumes, or which rule is tried next. There is no
mechanism in the PEG layers by which an action could refuse a match or
request more input. Semantic actions are observers with side effects, not
participants in the parse.

\section{Actions and the Other Rule Hooks}

The action is not the only way a rule can be given behaviour, and the two
other hooks interact with it in ways that are easy to get wrong.

\code{fire_on_neg} decides whether the action of a negated rule runs. By
default it does not: a negated rule that succeeds has matched precisely
because its pattern did \emph{not} match, and the text it has consumed is
the single character the driver took to guarantee progress, so reporting it
as a token would usually be wrong. Setting \code{fire_on_neg} makes the
action run anyway, which is what Listing~\ref{lst:ch25-negated} does in
order to report the character that a negated rule skipped over.

\code{if_succeed} is a pointer to a second rule that is attempted at the
same position once the first has matched, and whose own action runs if it
matches. Two consequences follow. The second rule is attempted at the
position of the first, not at the end of the first rule's match, so it sees
the same text; and the second rule's match does not advance the cursor, so
the pair consumes exactly what the first rule consumed. In effect
\code{if_succeed} is a zero-width secondary observation, and it is the
mechanism to use when one piece of text needs to be reported under two
different labels -- a keyword that is also a valid identifier, say.

Neither hook changes the matching behaviour, and neither can make a rule
match that would not otherwise match. They are reporting hooks, and like
the action itself they observe the parse rather than participate in it.

\section{Building a Tree with a Stack}

The way to build a tree from a flat token stream is a stack discipline, and
the way to implement it in this layer is to close over the stack.

The pattern has two kinds of action. A \emph{terminal} action pushes a leaf
node. An \emph{operator} action pushes an operator marker. And a terminal
action that finds an operator marker on top of the stack pops the marker
and the left operand, and pushes the node that combines them. This is the
classic shift-reduce construction, and it produces a left-associative tree.

Listing~\ref{lst:ch27-tree} is a complete program that does this. The
grammar is the expression grammar \code{Expr := Term ("+" Term)*} over
tokens, and the result is a real \code{dsl::ASTNode} tree that is printed
with \code{dump()}.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Building an \code{ASTNode} tree from PEG matches. The stack is captured by every action, so it outlives any individual match.},label={lst:ch27-tree}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int
main ()
{
  // The semantic-value stack.  It is captured by every action, so it
  // outlives any single match.
  std::vector<dsl::ASTNode> stack;

  auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();

  {
    auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \t\n]+"> (
        [&stack] (dsl::PEGMatch &m)
        {
          // user_data is a scratch slot on this match only.
          m.user_data = &stack;
        });
    ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;
  }
  {
    grammar.add_rule<"[0-9]+"> (
        [&stack] (dsl::PEGMatch &m)
        {
          dsl::ASTNode rhs = dsl::leaf<"num">(m.value);
          if (!stack.empty () && stack.back ().tag () == "op")
            {
              dsl::ASTNode op = stack.back ();
              stack.pop_back ();
              dsl::ASTNode lhs = stack.back ();
              stack.pop_back ();
              std::string tag = (op.value () == "+") ? "add" : "mul";
              stack.push_back (dsl::ASTNode{ tag, { lhs, rhs } });
            }
          else
            stack.push_back (rhs);
        });
  }
  {
    grammar.add_rule<"+"> (
        [&stack] (dsl::PEGMatch &m)
        { stack.push_back (dsl::leaf<"op">(m.value)); });
  }
  {
    grammar.add_rule<"*"> (
        [&stack] (dsl::PEGMatch &m)
        { stack.push_back (dsl::leaf<"op">(m.value)); });
  }

  auto result = grammar.parse ("1 + 2 + 3 * 4");

  std::cout << "parse " << (result.ok () ? "succeeded" : "failed")
            << ", stack depth " << stack.size () << "\n";
  std::cout << "tree: " << stack.back ().dump () << "\n";

  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch27-tree}. The tree is left-associative, which is what the flat token stream can express.},label={lst:ch27-tree-out}]
parse succeeded, stack depth 1
tree: (mul (add (add (num 1) (num 2)) (num 3)) (num 4))
\end{lstlisting}

The tree is exactly what the grammar says. \code{Expr := Term ("+" Term)*}
with left recursion gives \code{(add (add (num 1) (num 2)) (num 3))}, and
the multiplication, which the same flat construction folds in the same
left-associative way, gives the outer \code{mul}. A grammar with
precedence -- \code{Expr := Term ("+" Term)*}, \code{Term := Factor ("*"
Factor)*} -- would require the reduction to happen in a different order,
and the flat token stream cannot express that. This is the point at which
the definition layer stops being enough, and the combinator layer of
\chapref{ch:peg-combinators} takes over: there, the recursion is written
out, and the tree falls out of the parser's own structure.

The mechanics of the construction are worth spelling out, because they are
the whole technique. The operator action pushes a node with the tag
\code{"op"} and the operator as its value; the number action inspects the
top of the stack, and if it finds an operator marker it pops the marker and
the operand below it and pushes the combined node. The distinction between
an operator marker and an operand is made by the tag, so the stack holds
only \code{ASTNode} values and no side table is needed.

The node is built with \code{dsl::ASTNode{tag, {lhs, rhs}}}, the
two-argument constructor that takes a tag and a vector of children. The
compile-time tagged factory \code{dsl::node<"add">(lhs, rhs)} cannot be used
here, because the tag is only known at run time: the grammar distinguishes
addition from multiplication by the text of the operator, and there is no
compile-time string to hand to the template.

The pseudocode below states the construction formally.

\begin{pseudocode}[Building a tree with a value stack]
\textbf{function} build (definition, text)\\
\quad stack = empty \quad // a stack of ASTNode\\
\quad \textbf{for each} match m \textbf{produced by} definition.parse(text) \textbf{do}\\
\qquad \textbf{if} m is an operator \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\quad push leaf("op", m.value) \textbf{onto} stack\\
\qquad \textbf{else} \textbf{if} the top of stack is an operator \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\quad op = pop(stack)\\
\qquad\quad lhs = pop(stack)\\
\qquad\quad push node(op.tag, lhs, leaf("num", m.value)) \textbf{onto} stack\\
\qquad \textbf{else}\\
\qquad\quad push leaf("num", m.value) \textbf{onto} stack\\
\quad \textbf{return} the single node left on stack
\end{pseudocode}

Figure~\ref{fig:ch27-stack} shows the stack evolving as the input
\code{1 + 2 + 3} is consumed.

