\section{The \code{user_data} Slot}
\label{sec:ch27-userdata}

The \code{user_data} member deserves a section of its own, because it is
the one part of the match record that a reader is most likely to
misunderstand.

The slot is there, it is writable, and it is null on entry to every action.
What it is not is a channel between matches. The definition layer builds a
fresh \code{PEGMatch} for each match, fills in the fields it cares about,
and hands it to the action; the match is then discarded when the action
returns. Nothing copies it, nothing stores it, and the next match starts
from a new record with \code{user_data} set to null. Writing a pointer
into the slot therefore has no effect beyond the duration of the action.

Listing~\ref{lst:ch27-userdata} demonstrates this directly.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The \code{user_data} slot is a per-match scratch pad. The pointer written by the first action is not visible to the second.},label={lst:ch27-userdata}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <iostream>

int
main ()
{
  int payload = 7;

  auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();

  {
    auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \t\n]+"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
    ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;
  }
  grammar.add_rule<"[a-z]+"> (
      [&] (dsl::PEGMatch &m)
      {
        std::cout << "first match, user_data = "
                  << (m.user_data ? "set" : "null") << "\n";
        m.user_data = &payload;
        std::cout << "first match, user_data = "
                  << (m.user_data ? "set" : "null") << " (written)\n";
      });
  grammar.add_rule<"[0-9]+"> (
      [&] (dsl::PEGMatch &m)
      {
        std::cout << "second match, user_data = "
                  << (m.user_data ? "set" : "null") << "\n";
      });

  auto result = grammar.parse ("ab 12");
  std::cout << "parse " << (result.ok () ? "succeeded" : "failed") << "\n";
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch27-userdata}.},label={lst:ch27-userdata-out}]
first match, user_data = null
first match, user_data = set (written)
second match, user_data = null
parse succeeded
\end{lstlisting}

The second action sees a null slot even though the first action wrote a
pointer into it. The value did not travel.

There is one place where a \code{PEGMatch} is copied and its
\code{user_data} travels with it: the combinator layer. A
\code{Parser<PEGMatch>} that produces a match, whose result is collected by
\code{peg_many} or \code{peg_many1}, produces a vector or a pair of
matches, and the slot is copied along with the rest of the record. A
hand-written parser that sets \code{m.user_data} to point at a node can
therefore propagate that node up through a repetition, and the consumer can
recover the tree from the vector. This is the closest the toolkit comes to a
typed semantic value in the PEG layers, and it is worth knowing about, but
it is an accident of the copying rather than a designed facility.

The practical consequence for tree building is simple: keep the semantic
state in a variable that the action closes over, as
Listing~\ref{lst:ch27-tree} does, and treat \code{user_data} as a scratch
pad for values that are consumed within a single action.

\section{Three Ways to Build a Tree}

It is worth setting the PEG action model against the other two ways
\DSLtk{} can produce a tree, because the differences are not ones of taste.

A hand-written recursive-descent parser over \code{dsl::Parser<T>} makes
the semantic value the parser's result type. The parser for a rule returns
the subtree that the rule denotes, and the rule's parser combines the
subtrees of its parts. The value is typed, statically, all the way down;
there is no stack discipline to maintain by hand, and no tag strings to
compare at run time. The price is that every rule is a function, the
recursion is explicit, and the tree shape is whatever the composition of the
parsers says it is. This is the approach of \chapref{ch:parser-core} and
\chapref{ch:primitive-parsers}, and it is the right default.

The PEG action model inverts the responsibility. The matching layer knows
nothing about values; the user manufactures them from matches, usually with
a stack. The advantage is that the matching layer is simple and the actions
are ordinary C++ that can do anything. The cost is that the value has no
type, the tree shape is implicit in the order the actions happen to fire,
and the whole construction is a protocol between the grammar and the
actions that only the author of both knows about.

The Earley parser of \chapref{ch:cfg-earley} takes the third route. Its
input is a vector of tokens rather than a string, it accepts a genuine
context-free grammar with left recursion and ambiguity, and it returns the
tree directly: \code{CFG::parse} fills in a \code{CFGParseResult} whose
\code{tree} member is a \code{std::optional<ASTNode>}. The tree is built by
the algorithm itself, because the algorithm keeps the partial derivations
and the tree is what falls out of them. It handles far more grammars than
either of the other two, and it costs more: the chart is quadratic in the
worst case, the token vector must be built by a lexer you write yourself,
and for an unambiguous grammar the Earley machinery is doing far more work
than a recursive-descent parser would.

The three routes are not exclusive, and the toolkit's own examples mix
them. A natural pipeline for a language with a real grammar is to use the
definition layer as a lexer -- it is fast, it is linear, and it produces
tokens with positions -- to hand the resulting token vector to the Earley
parser of \chapref{ch:cfg-earley}, which wants tokens rather than
characters, and to keep the PEG actions for whatever the tokens mean
outside the grammar. Another natural pipeline is to use the definition
layer for the tokens and the combinator layer for the expression grammar on
top of them, as \chapref{ch:peg-combinators} does; there the tree is
assembled by the structure of the parser rather than by a stack discipline,
and the actions of the definition layer are left with the job of
classifying tokens.

