\section{Edge Cases and Limits}

\paragraph{An action cannot influence the match.} The action fires after
the match has been decided, the span has been fixed and the cursor has been
advanced. In the streaming matcher the cursor is set before the action
runs, so even a change to \code{m.end} has no effect on where the next
match starts. There is no mechanism for an action to reject a match, to
consume more input, or to request a different rule.

\paragraph{\code{value} is a view, and it is easy to dangle.} The view
points into the buffer the caller supplied to \code{parse}. If that buffer
is a temporary, the view is dead as soon as the parse returns, and a tree
that stored it would store a dangling pointer. Copy the text into the tree.

\paragraph{\code{user_data} does not carry anything.} It is null on entry
to every action and is never copied to the next match. See
Section~\ref{sec:ch27-userdata}. Semantic state that must outlive a match
belongs in a variable the action closes over.

\paragraph{There is no left recursion.} Neither PEG layer can express a
rule that refers to itself at the left, because matching is a linear scan
with no recursion at all. A grammar such as \code{Expr := Expr "+" Term}
cannot be written, and \code{Expr := Term ("+" Term)*} is the standard
workaround -- with the consequence that the resulting tree is
left-associative and that operator precedence must be encoded by the order
of the reductions rather than by the grammar.

\paragraph{The tree shape is a convention, not a guarantee.} Nothing in the
subsystem checks that the actions of a grammar agree with each other. If
one action pushes two nodes and another pops one, the stack is corrupted
and the failure appears much later, as a malformed tree or an out-of-range
access. The layer provides no consistency checking, and a malformed stack
is not diagnosed at all.

\paragraph{The rule pointer is unstable.} The pointer in
\code{PEGMatch::rule} points into the definition's vector of rules, and
that vector may be reallocated by a later \code{add_rule} call, as
described in \chapref{ch:peg-definition}. An action that compares
\code{m.rule} against a captured rule pointer is comparing against a
pointer that may no longer designate that rule.

\paragraph{Only the flat token stream is available.} The definition layer
sees one token at a time, with no structure above it. A tree with more than
one level of nesting has to be reconstructed by the actions, and the shape
of the reconstruction is limited by the order in which the tokens arrive.
A grammar with real precedence needs the combinator layer, where the
recursion is written out.

\paragraph{Positions are recomputed from the start.} The line and column
of a match are computed by scanning the source text from its beginning, so
a long input costs a quadratic amount of work in the position arithmetic
alone, before any action runs.

\paragraph{There is no error recovery.} An action cannot fail a parse, and
there is no mechanism for skipping a bad token and carrying on. A parse
that fails has failed, and whatever the actions built up to that point is
discarded unless the caller kept it.

\section{Closing}

The PEG layers give you matches and spans and leave the tree to you. That
separation is a real virtue in a lexer, where the tree is often not wanted
at all, and a real limitation in a parser, where it usually is. The stack
discipline of Listing~\ref{lst:ch27-tree} is the idiom that closes the gap:
push terminals, push operators, reduce on the next terminal, and the tree
assembles itself as the tokens arrive.

The two things to keep in mind are the two limits that the design imposes
rather than the programmer. An action observes; it does not participate. And
the value a match carries is a view into somebody else's buffer, not a
piece of the tree. Everything else -- how to type the values, how to shape
the tree, how to get left recursion -- is a choice the user makes, and the
three routes through \DSLtk{} make different choices.
